        <p class="story-kicker">SCIENCE</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Study Finds Average Meeting Could Have Been Email and Also Wasn&#x27;t</h1>
        <p class="article-dek">Researchers describe paradox as &#x27;the Schrödinger stand-up.</p>
        <p class="article-meta">By Dr. Helen Marsh · Wednesday, August 19, 2026 · BOSTON · 2 min read</p>
        
<div class="article-share" aria-label="Share this story">
  <span class="article-share-label">Share</span>
  <a href="mailto:?subject=Study%20Finds%20Average%20Meeting%20Could%20Have%20Been%20Email%20and%20Also%20Wasn%27t&amp;body=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/study-finds-average-meeting-could-have-been-email-and-also-wasnt/">Email</a>
  <a href="https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/study-finds-average-meeting-could-have-been-email-and-also-wasnt/&amp;text=Study%20Finds%20Average%20Meeting%20Could%20Have%20Been%20Email%20and%20Also%20Wasn%27t" rel="noopener">Post</a>
  <a href="../../reprints.html">Reprints</a>
</div>
        
        <figure class="article-hero article-hero-fit-cover">
          <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1553877522-43269d4ea984?auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;w=800&amp;h=500&amp;q=80" alt="" />
        </figure>
        
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>BOSTON - MIT researchers published a paper Monday demonstrating that 68% of workplace meetings simultaneously could have been emails and also weren&#x27;t until observed by a manager entering the room, according to findings from an extensive study involving 200 conference rooms instrumented with microphones and guilt sensors.</p>
<p>&quot;The moment leadership joins, the meeting collapses into necessity,&quot; said lead author Dr. Stephen Voss. &quot;Before that, it&#x27;s quantum fluff.&quot; The team found that peak productivity occurred during the two minutes before start time when attendees almost sent the email but didn&#x27;t, fearing it seemed rude. This phenomenon explained why calendars fill with recurring syncs that exist “just to align,” yet never do.</p>
<p>The study’s findings were met with skepticism from corporate clients. &quot;Email lacks synergy,&quot; said CloudPath executive Dana Hooper, scheduling a meeting to discuss the study. She added, &quot;We need to see each other&#x27;s faces and hear each other&#x27;s voices.&quot;</p>
<p>The paper introduced the concept of the Schrödinger stand-up: a daily ritual where teams report blockers that were blockers only because nobody replied in Slack. “It’s like saying, ‘I’m blocked by an invisible wall until I can finally break through it,’&quot; explained Voss.</p>
<p>Voss proposed replacing half of all meetings with structured documents and the other half with honest admission that people like hearing themselves talk. &quot;We need to be more transparent about why we&#x27;re meeting,&quot; he said. &quot;If the meeting lacks decisions, food, or a legal requirement, send the email and touch grass per CDC guidance.&quot;</p>
<p>Corporate clients were resistant. “Email is just not as effective,” argued HR Director Lisa Johnson of TechCorp. “We need face-to-face interaction to build trust and rapport.”</p>
<p>The paper also introduced the concept of the &quot;quantum fluff&quot; meeting: a gathering that exists in a state of potential until observed by leadership, at which point it becomes necessary. Voss recommended a simple test for identifying such meetings: if they lack decisions, food, or legal requirement, send an email and touch grass according to CDC guidelines.</p>
<p>He acknowledged the advice may itself require a meeting to implement. “It’s ironic,” said Voss with a straight face. “But we have to start somewhere.”</p>
<p>The study&#x27;s findings were met with mixed reactions from employees. &quot;I&#x27;m all for saving time,&quot; said software engineer Alex Chen, &quot;but I don&#x27;t want to feel like I’m missing out on something important just because I didn’t get an email.&quot;</p>
<p>The team’s next steps include developing a tool that can automatically detect unnecessary meetings and suggest alternative communication methods. “We’re working on a plugin for Slack that will flag meetings as quantum fluff,” said Voss, adding, &quot;It&#x27;s not easy to change decades of corporate culture, but we&#x27;re making progress.&quot;</p>
          
        </div>
        
